<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Sign-in dialog. The layout is built around the CODE, not the QR image.

    The provider does not return `verification_uri_complete`, measured in all
    three spike runs, so the QR can only ever carry the address - never the
    code. A layout that gives the QR the left third and leaves the code inside
    a body block is therefore the wrong shape: the code is the thing a person
    has to read across a room and type on a phone, and the QR is a convenience
    that saves them typing the address. The code gets the full panel width and
    its own control; the QR is sized by what is left over.

    Fonts must be names the ACTIVE SKIN defines. Kodi falls back to font13
    silently when it cannot resolve a font name - no error, no log line - which
    is how this file shipped naming `font12_title`, a font Estuary does not
    define, with its body text rendering at the fallback ever since.

    THE CODE FONT, and the chain behind it.

    Measured from skin.estuary's xml/Font.xml on the Omega (Kodi 21) branch of
    xbmc/xbmc - these are sizes read out of that file, not sizes recalled:

        WeatherTemp    120   <- named below by control 1005
        font_clock      70
        font60          60
        font52_title    52
        font45          45

    Read downward, that is the order to walk if a real code proves too wide or
    too tall on a television. Read upward, it is the answer to AUTH-05, which
    asks for "the largest font the skin offers": 120 is the largest size
    Estuary defines anywhere, so on the stock skin the top of the chain is the
    literal answer and there is nothing above it to reach for.

    This supersedes the instruction that stood here between 345999a and now,
    which named font60 and said "do NOT reach for WeatherTemp". The caution
    behind that instruction is still true and is restated in the next
    paragraph; only the verdict changed. font60 was read on a TCL Android TV 12
    (Kodi 21.2, stock Estuary) during the 03-14 acceptance run and reported too
    small from a normal seat, with exactly 2x of unused headroom above it.

    Kodi's skin XML has NO font-fallback operator: one name is named, and it
    either resolves or silently becomes font13. So the chain above is a chain
    for a human reader, not for the renderer. Of the five names, the lower four
    are ordinary Estuary names; `WeatherTemp` is special-purpose - it exists to
    put a temperature on a weather pane - and it is therefore the one of the
    five most likely to be ABSENT from a third-party skin, where it will
    substitute down to font13 (30px) without a word in the log. If this dialog
    is ever reported tiny on a non-stock skin, that is the first thing to
    suspect, and the repair is to step down to font60, which skins define in
    practice.

    Nothing in this repository can tell you which of those two happened. A gate
    can read the name out of this file, check it against the recorded chain,
    and check the box around it is big enough to hold the glyphs. Only a person
    looking at a screen can say what rendered. That distinction is exactly why
    AUTH-05 forbids recording the requirement as met on the strength of this
    file. The gates are in tests/test_auth_gates.py under "the code font".

    Box sizing. At 120px the Estuary face runs about 71px per uppercase
    alphanumeric - from 03-06's measurement of ~320px for nine characters at
    60px, i.e. 0.59em - so the 1150-wide control holds sixteen characters and
    the provider's codes are eight or nine. The 152-high box clears a 120px
    line height with room over. A 120px glyph in the 96-high box this control
    used to have would have been clipped top and bottom, and a clipped code is
    worse than a small one.
-->
<window>
    <coordinates>
        <!-- The panel grew from 450 to 750 to seat a 120px code, a body block
             that no longer overflows, and a larger QR. 165 keeps it centred in
             the 1080-line space: (1080 - 750) / 2. Bottom edge 915. -->
        <left>385</left>
        <top>165</top>
    </coordinates>
    <!-- The only input device is a directional pad, so the initial focus is a
         decision rather than an accident. Cancel takes it: the second button
         is hidden until the code expires. -->
    <defaultcontrol always="true">1003</defaultcontrol>
    <controls>
        <animation type="WindowOpen" reversible="false">
            <effect type="zoom" start="80" end="100" center="960,540" delay="160" tween="back" time="240" />
            <effect type="fade" delay="160" end="100" time="240" />
        </animation>
        <animation type="WindowClose" reversible="false">
            <effect type="zoom" start="100" end="80" center="960,540" easing="in" tween="back" time="240" />
            <effect type="fade" start="100" end="0" time="240" />
        </animation>
        <control type="image">
            <left>-1920</left>
            <top>-1080</top>
            <width>5760</width>
            <height>3240</height>
            <animation effect="fade" start="0" end="100" time="300">WindowOpen</animation>
            <animation effect="fade" start="100" end="0" time="200">WindowClose</animation>
            <texture colordiffuse="C2FFFFFF">black.png</texture>
        </control>
        <!-- Dialog panel -->
        <control type="image">
            <left>0</left>
            <top>0</top>
            <width>1150</width>
            <height>750</height>
            <texture border="2">dialog-bg.png</texture>
        </control>
        <!-- Title bar -->
        <control type="image">
            <left>0</left>
            <top>0</top>
            <width>1150</width>
            <height>70</height>
            <texture colordiffuse="FF147A96" border="2">white.png</texture>
        </control>
        <!-- Title label -->
        <control type="label" id="1000">
            <textoffsetx>20</textoffsetx>
            <left>0</left>
            <top>0</top>
            <width>1150</width>
            <height>70</height>
            <font>font30_title</font>
            <align>left</align>
            <aligny>center</aligny>
            <shadowcolor>FF000000</shadowcolor>
        </control>
        <!-- The code. The largest element on the panel, and the reason the
             panel exists. WeatherTemp is 120px and this box is 152 high, so
             the glyphs cannot clip. Filled from Python; see
             QRDialogProgress.set_code. Read the header before changing the
             font name: there is a measured chain to step down, and no
             fallback the renderer will apply on your behalf. -->
        <control type="label" id="1005">
            <left>0</left>
            <top>78</top>
            <width>1150</width>
            <height>152</height>
            <font>WeatherTemp</font>
            <textcolor>FFFFFFFF</textcolor>
            <align>center</align>
            <aligny>center</aligny>
            <shadowcolor>FF000000</shadowcolor>
        </control>
        <!-- QR image, still secondary: it carries the address only, for the
             reason at the top of this file. 150x150 was smaller than it needed
             to be. 280 is what the width allows, and the width is what binds:
             the panel is 1150, the text column needs 798 to hold the address
             on one unwrapped line at font37, and 24 of padding each side plus
             a 24 gutter leaves exactly 280. Top 292 centres it in the 364-high
             body row. -->
        <control type="image" id="1001">
            <left>24</left>
            <top>292</top>
            <width>280</width>
            <height>280</height>
            <aspectratio aligny="center" align="center">keep</aspectratio>
        </control>
        <!-- Text panel: the instruction, the address, and the countdown, in
             that order, joined by the dialog's _render_text.

             font37 rather than font27, because the last of those three lines
             is the expiry countdown and it is read from the same seat as the
             code. The height went with it, and by more than the font did: at
             font27 in a 156-high box this control was ALREADY overflowing -
             five wrapped lines of roughly 38px against 156 of room - so the
             countdown was the line being pushed out of view. At font37 the
             same content wraps to six lines of roughly 52px, which is 312 in a
             box of 364. The line left spare is the margin a longer
             translation gets to use. -->
        <control type="textbox" id="1002">
            <left>328</left>
            <top>250</top>
            <width>798</width>
            <height>364</height>
            <font>font37</font>
            <align>left</align>
            <aligny>top</aligny>
            <shadowcolor>FF000000</shadowcolor>
        </control>
        <!-- Get a new code. Hidden until the code expires, then shown and
             focused exactly once by QRDialogProgress.set_expired. Its label is
             set from Python so the words live in the catalogue, not here. -->
        <control type="button" id="1004">
            <left>520</left>
            <top>630</top>
            <width>300</width>
            <height>96</height>
            <font>font25_title</font>
            <textcolor>FFFFFFFF</textcolor>
            <align>center</align>
            <aligny>center</aligny>

            <onup>1004</onup>
            <ondown>1004</ondown>
            <onleft>1003</onleft>
            <onright>1003</onright>
        </control>
        <!-- Cancel button. Stays present through the expiry transition, so
             there are always two ways out of this dialog. -->
        <control type="button" id="1003">
            <left>840</left>
            <top>630</top>
            <width>290</width>
            <height>96</height>
            <font>font25_title</font>
            <textcolor>FFFFFFFF</textcolor>
            <label>222</label>
            <align>center</align>
            <aligny>center</aligny>

            <onup>1003</onup>
            <ondown>1003</ondown>
            <onleft>1004</onleft>
            <onright>1004</onright>
        </control>
    </controls>
</window>
